                Blocks(
                    Paragraph(Text("Let L(0), L(1), L(2) be arbitrary finite nontrivial commutative rings without zero divisors, let R be a finite commutative ring, and specify a ring equivalence from R to their product. Set a=card(L(0))-1, b=card(L(1))-1, c=card(L(2))-1 and y=1+X. On the entire actual vertex domain, the native polynomial is y^(ab+ac+bc) + (y^(a+ab+ac)-y^(ab+ac)) + (y^(b+ab+bc)-y^(ab+bc)) + (y^(c+ac+bc)-y^(ac+bc)).")),
                    Paragraph(Text("The proof partitions each actual vertex domain by its nonempty proper support and uses the inverse coordinate-choice bijection to evaluate its fibers. The six supports on three coordinates give pair-domain size ab+ac+bc, and coordinate-i star sizes a+ab+ac, b+ab+bc, c+ac+bc with the corresponding singleton cells removed for the tail domains. This evaluates Rather's Theorem 4.1 formula on actual ring elements. For distinct primes p,q,r the native CRT gives a=p-1, b=q-1, c=r-1, including p=2; no prime upper bound is introduced."))),
                DescribeRole.Theorem),

            Paragraph(Text("The support identity is used with the uniform coefficient inequalities below. Positivity and the zero tail identify and attain the actual independence number.")),

            Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create("nine-restriction-count"),
                DeclarationHandle.Create(Prefix + "nine_restriction_count"),
                H("Counting extensions of a selected prefix and pair"),
                StatementSource.FromAuthor(F.Disp(F.Seq(F.Forall,F.Sp,F.Id("m"),F.Sp,F.Id("n"),F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Colon,F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Mathbb,F.Grp(F.Id("N"))),F.Comma,F.Sp,F.Open,F.D(3),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Land,F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.D(5),F.Close,F.Sp,F.To,F.Sp,F.Sum,F.Sp,F.Id("j"),F.Sp,F.InMacro,F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("range"))),F.Open,F.D(1,0),F.Close,F.Comma,F.Sp,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("j"),F.Close,F.Cdot,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("n"),F.Comma,F.D(9),F.Minus,F.Id("j"),F.Close,F.Cdot,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("j"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Minus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Cdot,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.D(9),F.Minus,F.Id("j"),F.Comma,F.D(2),F.Close,F.Sp,F.Eq,F.Sp,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Minus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Cdot,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("n"),F.Comma,F.D(2),F.Close,F.Cdot,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Plus,F.Id("n"),F.Minus,F.Open,F.Id("k"),F.Plus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Comma,F.D(9),F.Minus,F.Open,F.Id("k"),F.Plus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Close))), AssessedProvenance.FromRepo(),
                Blocks(Paragraph(Text("For arbitrary natural block sizes m and n and rank k between three and five, sum over j from zero through nine the product choose(m,j) choose(n,9-j) choose(j,k-1) choose(9-j,2). This equals choose(m,k-1) choose(n,2) choose(m+n-(k+1),9-(k+1)). All subtractions here are natural subtractions, and binomial coefficients vanish outside their natural support.")),
                    Paragraph(Text("Only k-1 through seven can contribute. Shifting by k-1 and applying the composed-choice identity in each block leaves Vandermonde convolution on the remaining elements. If either block cannot supply its selected elements, both sides vanish. This counts extensions in arbitrary populations, rather than only a population of size nine."))),
                DescribeRole.Theorem),

