                Blocks(Paragraph(Text("For every natural N at least nine, m at most N and k from three through five, define H(N,m,k) as choose(m,k-1) choose(N-m,2) divided by choose(N,k+1) choose(k+1,2), with rational division. Then H(N,m,k) equals the sum over zero through nine of w(j) H(9,j,k), where w(j) is choose(m,j) choose(N-m,9-j) divided by choose(N,9).")),
                    Paragraph(Text("Every denominator is positive: k+1 lies between four and six and is at most nine and N. The integer extension count and the composed-choice identity cancel the number of extensions of a selected (k+1)-subset to a nine-subset."))),
                DescribeRole.Theorem),

            Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create("nine-restriction-bound"),
                DeclarationHandle.Create(Prefix + "nine_restriction_bound"),
                H("Uniform small-rank bounds"),
                StatementSource.FromAuthor(F.Disp(F.Seq(F.Forall,F.Sp,F.Id("N"),F.Sp,F.Id("m"),F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Colon,F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Mathbb,F.Grp(F.Id("N"))),F.Comma,F.Sp,F.Open,F.D(9),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Id("N"),F.Sp,F.Land,F.Sp,F.Id("m"),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Id("N"),F.Sp,F.Land,F.Sp,F.D(3),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Land,F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.D(5),F.Close,F.Sp,F.To,F.Sp,F.Id("H"),F.Open,F.Id("N"),F.Comma,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Close,F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Open,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("if"))),F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Eq,F.D(3),F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("then"))),F.Sp,F.D(5),F.Slash,F.D(6,3),F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("else"))),F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("if"))),F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Eq,F.D(4),F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("then"))),F.Sp,F.D(1),F.Slash,F.D(2,1),F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Operatorname,F.Grp(F.Id("else"))),F.Sp,F.D(1),F.Slash,F.D(2,8),F.Close))), AssessedProvenance.FromRepo(),
                Blocks(Paragraph(Text("For every N at least nine and m at most N, the rational quantity H(N,m,k) is at most 5/63 for k=3, at most 1/21 for k=4, and at most 1/28 for k=5. The theorem states these three values as one rank-indexed bound.")),
                    Paragraph(Text("The weights are nonnegative, and Vandermonde convolution shows that they sum to one. For j from zero through nine the numerators choose(j,k-1) choose(9-j,2) have respective maxima 60, 60 and 45. Their denominators are 756, 1260 and 1260. Applying these finite bounds inside the exact restriction average proves the stated bounds for all N. Three such nonnegative contributions are consequently bounded by 5/21, 1/7 and 3/28, respectively."))),
                DescribeRole.Theorem),

            Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create("normalized-second-difference"),
                DeclarationHandle.Create(Prefix + "normalized_second_difference"),
                H("Exact second difference"),
                StatementSource.FromAuthor(F.Disp(F.Seq(F.Forall,F.Sp,F.Id("N"),F.Sp,F.Id("m"),F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Colon,F.Sp,F.Seq(F.Mathbb,F.Grp(F.Id("N"))),F.Comma,F.Sp,F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Id("N"),F.Sp,F.Land,F.Sp,F.D(1),F.Sp,F.Leq,F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Land,F.Sp,F.Id("k"),F.Sp,F.Lt,F.Sp,F.Id("N"),F.Close,F.Sp,F.To,F.Sp,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Minus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Slash,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("N"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Minus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Sp,F.Minus,F.Sp,F.D(2),F.Cdot,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Close,F.Slash,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("N"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Close,F.Sp,F.Plus,F.Sp,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Plus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Slash,F.Id("B"),F.Open,F.Id("N"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Plus,F.D(1),F.Close,F.Sp,F.Eq,F.Sp,F.Id("H"),F.Open,F.Id("N"),F.Comma,F.Id("m"),F.Comma,F.Id("k"),F.Close))), AssessedProvenance.FromRepo(),
                Blocks(Paragraph(Text("Let N, m and k be natural numbers with m at most N and 1 at most k less than N. Write h(j)=choose(m,j)/choose(N,j), using rational division. Then h(k-1)-2h(k)+h(k+1) equals H(N,m,k)=choose(m,k-1) choose(N-m,2) divided by choose(N,k+1) choose(k+1,2). All binomial coefficients and their indices use natural numbers, so a coefficient with its lower index above its upper index is zero.")),
                    Paragraph(Text("The adjacent-coefficient recurrence, interpreted over the rationals, remains valid even beyond the natural support because both coefficients vanish. Apply it twice in each row and use choose(a,2)=a(a-1)/2 over the rationals. The resulting numerator factors as choose(m,k-1)(N-m)(N-m-1). The denominators are positive on the stated interval. This includes k=N-1, m below k-1, m=N and N-m below two."))),
                DescribeRole.Theorem),

